<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Hosts the real API request pipeline in process, which is why the API's Program class is public and
    partial in the global namespace. Every test class here must carry [Trait("Category", "Integration")],
    because that is what the "Category=Integration" filter selects.

    ⚠ NOT EVERY SUITE HERE STARTS A HOST, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN UNTIDY. Security/,
    Infrastructure/, Services/ and Validation/ hold suites whose subject is a single internal
    Infrastructure component driven with SUBSTITUTED collaborators - a recording logger, a mutable clock,
    a fabricated provider fault - which is a fact no resolved instance can express, because the
    substitution IS the fact. They live in this project because AAP 0.5.2.2 gives DnnMigration.UnitTests
    a single edge to Application, so a suite there cannot name an Infrastructure type at all; the
    Infrastructure assembly is reachable from here through this project's own single edge to the API, and
    that assembly's InternalsVisibleTo grant names THIS assembly. They carry the Integration trait like
    every other class here, which is correct: they run against production types rather than against
    contracts, and the filter that selects them is the one that selects everything in this project.

    Test-database strategy: every run uses a real SQL Server, and TestDatabaseFactory needs NO PREPARATION
    to find one, because the acceptance gates are run verbatim. Order: a truthy DNN_TESTS_USE_MSSQL starts
    a throwaway container; otherwise DNN_TEST_SQLSERVER names an existing server; otherwise a falsey
    DNN_TESTS_USE_MSSQL fails closed; otherwise a reachable container runtime is discovered and used;
    otherwise the run fails closed with one actionable diagnosis.

    No fixture selects the InMemory or Sqlite provider, and none can: the credential store answers
    "unavailable" on any provider that is not SQL Server, the accounts these tests sign in as live in the
    external aspnet_* tables no model-driven creation could produce, a query root arrives through
    FromSqlInterpolated, and the persistence suite asserts declared column types against the relational
    catalogue. Both references are retained because AAP 0.6.1 approves them, but they are deliberately
    unexercised. Recorded in MIGRATION_NOTES.md.

    Analyzer hazard: xunit.analyzers ships transitively with xunit and its xUnit#### rules are warnings,
    which the inherited warnings-as-errors policy promotes to build errors. Fix them in the test - never add
    an xUnit#### code to a NoWarn property, to backend/.editorconfig or to a new config file. The one that
    bites most often is the ban on blocking a Task inside a test, so every test method is `async Task`,
    never `async void`.

    Only one provider may be applied to a single DbContextOptions, so a fixture must REMOVE the existing
    provider registration before adding its own rather than layering one on top.

    The package list is closed: it is the set AAP 0.6.1 approves, plus one security pin.

    SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3 is pinned to 2.1.12 to clear CVE-2025-6965 (GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q, high),
    which covers SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 through 2.1.11; Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite 8.0.29
    selects the vulnerable 2.1.6 and no patched release exists on that line. The bundle is pinned rather
    than lib.e_sqlite3 alone so core, provider and lib move together and the native family cannot skew. A
    direct PackageReference wins resolution outright, which is safe only because nothing opens a SQLite
    connection - no UseSqlite call exists anywhere in the repository, so the native asset is never loaded.
    If a fixture ever does adopt Sqlite, re-validate this pin against it and prefer aligning the EF Core
    version over deepening the pin.

    Testcontainers.MsSql stays at the plan's 3.10.0 rather than 4.13.0, which AAP 0.6.4 lists among the
    versions checked and rejected; the 3.10.0 route was verified at runtime against this host's Docker
    Engine rather than assumed compatible.

    Microsoft.Data.SqlClient carries a direct pin at 5.2.3 (AAP 0.6.1) because without it the SQL Server
    provider resolves an older 5.1.x line transitively. Its reasoning sits beside the reference in
    DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="8.0.29" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.12" />
    <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.MsSql" Version="3.10.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The three schema scripts are embedded rather than copied so that the suite carries its
    own database definition inside the test assembly and cannot be run against a
    half-provisioned directory. DnnSchema.sql holds the mapped legacy tables;
    MembershipSchema.sql holds the external aspnet_* objects that no mapped entity type
    describes and that AAP 0.7.1.3 shows the legacy DDL chain only ever ALTERs;
    HostSettingsSchema.sql holds the HostSettings table, which is likewise unmapped and is
    read through IHostSettingsService rather than through an entity type.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\DnnSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\MembershipSchema.sql" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\HostSettingsSchema.sql" />
    <!--
      TerminalSchema.manifest is not provisioning DDL: it is the INDEPENDENT ORACLE the three
      scripts above are measured against, derived from the legacy upgrade chain rather than from
      anything this solution emits. It is embedded so that the comparison cannot silently degrade
      into "no manifest, no assertion" - TerminalSchema refuses to load without it.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Schema\TerminalSchema.manifest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly one project reference: Application, Infrastructure and Domain arrive
    transitively through the API, and naming them would blur the dependency
    direction the reference graph exists to enforce.

    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing supplies the ASP.NET Core framework
    reference needed to host the API in process, so this project stays on
    Microsoft.NET.Sdk and adds no FrameworkReference of its own.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DnnMigration.Api\DnnMigration.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
